\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The matched Wilson and Villain laws differ}
\date{2026-11-02}

\p{A single #{U(1)} plaquette has the
[normalized Wilson or Villain weight](fdip-007D). Matching their
fundamental Fourier coefficients determines a Villain coupling for
every Wilson coupling. The two probability laws still differ.}

\p{For #{\beta>0}, write #{r(\beta)=I_1(\beta)/I_0(\beta)} and
#{\beta_V(\beta)=-\frac{1}{2\log r(\beta)}}. Then #{0<r(\beta)<1},
the matched Villain coefficient is #{r(\beta)^{n^2}}, and
##{\frac{\widehat c_n^W}{\widehat c_n^V}\to\infty}
as #{|n|\to\infty}. In particular the two normalized weights are
distinct, so they define distinct probability laws on the circle.
The second Wilson coefficient is #{1-\frac{2r(\beta)}{\beta}}.}
\proof{
\p{The series for #{I_1} has positive terms, so #{r>0}. With normalized
Haar measure,
##{\int(1-\cos\theta)\,e^{\beta\cos\theta}\,\frac{d\theta}{2\pi}
=I_0(\beta)-I_1(\beta).}
The integrand is nonnegative and not identically zero, and the
exponential is positive, so the integral is positive. Thus #{r<1},
the logarithm is negative, and #{\beta_V} is positive. The Villain
normalization in the coefficient note gives
#{\widehat c_n^V=\exp(-n^2/(2\beta_V))}. Substituting the displayed
#{\beta_V} produces #{1/(2\beta_V)=-\log r}, hence
#{\widehat c_n^V=r^{n^2}}.}

\p{The #{j=0} term of the series for #{I_n(\beta)}, with #{n\ge 0}, is
#{(\beta/2)^n/n!}. Every term is positive, so
#{I_n(\beta)\ge(\beta/2)^n/n!}. Therefore
#{\widehat c_n^W=I_n(\beta)/I_0(\beta)} satisfies
##{\frac{\widehat c_n^W}{r^{n^2}}
\ge\frac{(\beta/2)^n}{n!\,I_0(\beta)\,r^{n^2}}.}
For #{n\ge 1}, one has #{n!\le n^n}, so the logarithm of the right-hand
side is at least
#{n\log(\beta/2)-n\log n-\log I_0(\beta)-n^2\log r}.
The last summand tends to #{+\infty} because #{\log r<0}, and it
dominates the remaining terms. The lower bound tends to infinity, and
so does the ratio, as #{n\to+\infty}. The series in the coefficient
note depends on #{|n|}, so #{I_n(\beta)=I_{-n}(\beta)}. The matched
Villain coefficient #{r^{n^2}} is likewise unchanged when #{n} is
replaced by #{-n}. The ratio at #{-n} equals the ratio at #{n}, and
the same limit holds as #{|n|\to\infty}.}

\p{The coefficient note identifies each normalized continuous weight with
its Fourier series. Some coefficient of large order differs, so the
weights differ as functions and as probability laws.}

\p{The same series gives the second coefficient. The identity
#{\frac{2}{\beta}(\beta/2)^{2j+1}=(\beta/2)^{2j}} rewrites
##{\frac{2}{\beta}I_1(\beta)
=\sum_{j\ge 0}\frac{(\beta/2)^{2j}}{j!\,(j+1)!}.}
Subtracting from #{I_0} cancels the #{j=0} term. For #{j\ge 1} the
difference of the summands is
#{\frac{(\beta/2)^{2j}\,j}{j!\,(j+1)!}
=\frac{(\beta/2)^{2j}}{(j-1)!(j+1)!}},
which is the #{j-1} term of #{I_2}. Hence
#{I_0-I_2=\frac{2}{\beta}I_1}, and
#{\widehat c_2^W=1-\frac{2r(\beta)}{\beta}}. At #{\beta=2} this
coefficient is #{1-r(2)}, while the matched Villain coefficient is
#{r(2)^4}.}
}

\p{The comparison is a one-plaquette identity. It does not assert a
many-plaquette continuum limit.}
